From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Norman Herms <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 20:48:05 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] clk: sunxi-ng: a733: Select PLL_REF as the input of the PLLs

All PLLs of the A733 CCU but PLL_REF have pll-ref, the 24 MHz output of
PLL_REF, as their parent, which is the reference clock the manual gives
for them (section 4.1.3.2, Table 4-2). Bit 24 of their control
registers selects that input. With the bit cleared the PLL runs from the
26 MHz DCXO, and every rate below it is 8.3 % higher than the clock
tree says. The manual lists the bit as reserved, and the reset values
it gives have it cleared (section 4.1.6). The hardware reads it set
before Linux starts; the driver relies on the boot firmware for that.

Seen on the Radxa Cubie A7S: with bit 24 of PLL_VIDEO2 cleared, a
clock-bound G2D copy from VIDEO2PLL4X took 24/26 of the time it took
with the bit set. Only with the bit set did the time match the rate
computed from 24 MHz.

Set the bit in probe, next to the enable bits, on PLL_PERI0/1, PLL_GPU0,
PLL_VIDEO0/1/2, PLL_VE0/1, PLL_AUDIO0/1, PLL_NPU and PLL_DE, the 24 MHz
column of the table without PLL_DDR. Where the bit is already set, the
write changes nothing.

This corrects "clk: sunxi-ng: a733: Add PLL clocks support" of the A733
CCU series v6.

Not covered: PLL_REF and the CPU PLLs (26 MHz in the table, bit 24
reads 0) are left alone. PLL_DDR belongs to the DRAM init and runs the
memory at probe time; switching its input would make the DRAM clock
jump, so it is left as found. Any other PLL that the boot firmware left
running from the DCXO changes its rate at this write.

Tested on Radxa Cubie A7S: with bit 24 of PLL_VIDEO1 and PLL_VIDEO2
cleared before the kernel started, probe set it again on every listed
PLL; without this patch it stayed cleared.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733.c b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733.c
index 16527f7747d7..ef14e4336af5 100644
--- a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733.c
@@ -2414,6 +2414,15 @@ static struct ccu_pll_nb sun60i_a733_pll_gpu0_nb = {
 	.delay_us	= 20,
 };
 
+/*
+ * Bit 24 of the PLL control registers selects the input of the PLL:
+ * 1 is the 24MHz output of PLL_REF, 0 the DCXO. The manual lists the bit
+ * as reserved. The PLLs below PLL_REF are modelled with pll-ref as their
+ * parent, so they need it set. PLL_DDR is left as the DRAM init set it:
+ * switching its input would make the clock of the running DRAM jump.
+ */
+#define SUN60I_A733_PLL_INPUT_PLL_REF	BIT(24)
+
 static const u32 pll_regs[] = {
 	SUN60I_A733_PLL_REF_REG,
 	SUN60I_A733_PLL_DDR_REG,
@@ -2449,6 +2458,9 @@ static int sun60i_a733_ccu_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(pll_regs); i++) {
 		val = readl(reg + pll_regs[i]);
 		val |= BIT(31) | BIT(30) | BIT(29);
+		if (pll_regs[i] != SUN60I_A733_PLL_REF_REG &&
+		    pll_regs[i] != SUN60I_A733_PLL_DDR_REG)
+			val |= SUN60I_A733_PLL_INPUT_PLL_REF;
 		writel(val, reg + pll_regs[i]);
 	}
 
